## The short version, before the detail

🔴 **The thing this case is named after -- a trap emitted because a temperature went out of
limits -- was NOT PRODUCED, and on this bench it cannot be.** Two independent reasons, both
measured here rather than assumed:

1. **No threshold can be moved to meet a reading.** `temperature ?` and `environment ?` are both
   `% Unrecognized command` in config mode (triage), `snmp-server enable trap ?` has no
   temperature group, and every limit in `show system environment` is reported by the hardware,
   not configured. The coolest margin on the box is **Temp: Front Right at 27 C against a 61 C
   limit**. Firing the real fault needs the unit physically heated past that, which a bench
   cannot command.
2. **The substitute the dispatch proposed is not possible on this product either.** The plan was
   an RMON alarm bound to the temperature sensor's AT-ENVMONv2 OID with a rising threshold just
   below the current reading. Attempted, in four syntaxes. **The DUT refuses all of them and says
   why in its own words** -- see STEP 4. An AW+ RMON alarm can be bound to the etherStats and
   etherHistory tables and to nothing else.

What that leaves, and what this case therefore grades on, is in STEPS 1-7: the trap group arms,
appears in running-config, chains with other groups, removes per-group and removes cleanly; the
temperature itself is fully exposed in the AT-ENVMONv2 MIB and agrees sensor for sensor with the
CLI; and, with the trap transport **proven live in the same capture window by a known-good RMON
trap**, no hardware-monitoring trap is emitted while all fourteen temperature sensors read Ok.

