## STEP 3 (designed) -- the temperature IS fully exposed over SNMP, and agrees with the CLI

The AT-ENVMONv2 MIB was located by walking the enterprise tree (`work/s2-envmon-find.log`) and
then walked in full: **`1.3.6.1.4.1.207.8.4.4.3.12`, 354 lines** (`work/s3-envmon-walk.log`).
Its shape, measured from the walk: `.12.1` fans (6 rows), `.12.2` voltage (14), **`.12.3`
temperature (14 rows -- exactly the fourteen sensors above)**, `.12.4` power/current.

The temperature table is `1.3.6.1.4.1.207.8.4.4.3.12.3.1.<col>.1.<resource>.<sensor>`:

| col | what it holds |
| --- | --- |
| 4 | the sensor description string |
| **5** | **the current reading, degrees C, INTEGER** |
| **6** | **the upper threshold, degrees C, INTEGER** |
| 7 | the status (2 on every row, i.e. Ok) |

Read back, every row (abridged -- the full 354-line walk is in `work/s3-envmon-walk.log`):

    .1.3.6.1.4.1.207.8.4.4.3.12.3.1.4.1.100.6  = STRING: "Temp: Front Right"
    .1.3.6.1.4.1.207.8.4.4.3.12.3.1.5.1.100.6  = INTEGER: 27        <- reading
    .1.3.6.1.4.1.207.8.4.4.3.12.3.1.6.1.100.6  = INTEGER: 61        <- limit
    .1.3.6.1.4.1.207.8.4.4.3.12.3.1.7.1.100.6  = INTEGER: 2         <- Ok
    ... 100.7 = 33/70, 100.8 = 29/77, 100.16 = 30/61, 100.17 = 34/67, 100.18 = 34/77,
        100.21 = 37/95, 100.22 = 40/95, 101.8 = 37/65, 101.9 = 70/105, 101.10 = 52/95,
        102.8 = 40/65, 102.9 = 51/105, 102.10 = 45/95

**Every reading and every limit is identical to the `show system environment` table in STEP 1**,
sensor for sensor, and every status column reads 2. (The two sensors that differ by one degree
between the two reads -- SoC AP 38/37, SoC SB 40/41 -- are the hardware actually drifting between
two reads a minute apart, which is the expected behaviour of a live sensor, not a mismatch.)

**This is the substantive positive result of the case.** A manager cannot be told by this box
that a temperature went out of range, but it can *ask*, over SNMP, and it gets the reading, the
threshold and the status in one row. A monitoring system can therefore threshold temperature
externally; what it cannot do is be notified by the device. **STEP 3 PASS.**

## STEP 4 (designed) -- 🔥 FINDING: an RMON alarm cannot be bound to anything outside RMON's own two tables

This is the step that decides the dispatch's limb (b). Four syntaxes for the same Front Right
sensor, plus a non-RMON standard OID, each sent separately so each error is its own evidence
(`work/s4-bind-attempts.log`). Events 21 and 22 were created first and were accepted:

    awplus(config)# rmon event 21 log trap testing123 description ck33413rise owner ck33413   [accepted]
    awplus(config)# rmon event 22 log trap testing123 description ck33413fall owner ck33413   [accepted]

    awplus(config)# rmon alarm 21 1.3.6.1.4.1.207.8.4.4.3.12.3.1.5.1.100.6 interval 10 absolute
                    rising-threshold 20 event 21 falling-threshold 1 event 22 owner ck33413
    % OID number did not match 1.3.6.1.2.1.16.1.1.1. (etherStats) or 1.3.6.1.2.1.16.2.2.1. (etherHistory)

    awplus(config)# rmon alarm 22 .1.3.6.1.4.1.207.8.4.4.3.12.3.1.5.1.100.6 interval 10 absolute ...
    % OID field string did not match an acceptable etherHistory field

    awplus(config)# rmon alarm 23 atEnvMonv2SensorTempValue.100.6 interval 10 absolute ...
    % OID field string did not match an acceptable etherHistory field

    awplus(config)# rmon alarm 24 envMonv2SensorValue.100.6 interval 10 absolute ...
    % OID field string did not match an acceptable etherHistory field

    awplus(config)# rmon alarm 25 sysUpTime.0 interval 10 absolute ...
    % Stats or history collection index was not correctly entered

`show rmon alarm` afterwards prints **nothing**: not one of the five was created.
`show running-config | include rmon` shows the two events and no alarm.

**The device states the restriction itself**, in the first message: an RMON alarm variable must
be a column of `1.3.6.1.2.1.16.1.1.1` (etherStatsEntry) or `1.3.6.1.2.1.16.2.2.1`
(etherHistoryEntry). RFC 2819 places no such limit on `alarmVariable` -- it is any integer-valued
MIB object -- and the whole point of the RMON alarm group is to let one watch *any* such object.
A threshold on an AT-ENVMONv2 temperature, a CPU gauge, a memory gauge or an interface counter
is therefore not expressible on this platform, even though all of those are readable from the
same agent (STEP 3 proves the temperature is).

**Consequence for this case:** limb (b) of the dispatch's plan -- "an RMON alarm bound to the
temperature sensor's AT-ENVMONv2 OID with a rising threshold just below the current reading,
producing a real trap derived from a real temperature value" -- **is not possible on this
product.** It is recorded here as a measured platform limitation with the device's own words as
evidence, not worked around. **STEP 4: the attempt FAILED as the product requires; the step
itself is a PASS as a measurement.**

## STEP 5 (designed) -- the negative control, with the trap transport proven live in the same window

The absence of a trap means nothing unless the path that would have carried it is known to work
at that moment (`/orient-dt` §4, the absence-of-evidence trap). So the capture window contains
BOTH the hardware-monitoring group, armed and watching fourteen healthy sensors, AND a
deliberately stimulated RMON alarm using the same community, the same trap host, the same UDP
port and the same receiver.

The control instrument (`work/s5-control-instrument.log`):

    awplus(config)# interface port1.0.10
    awplus(config-if)# rmon collection stats 11 owner ck33413
    awplus(config-if)# exit
    awplus(config)# rmon alarm 11 etherStatsEntry.5.11 interval 10 delta rising-threshold 100
                    event 21 falling-threshold 10 event 22 alarmstartup 1 owner ck33413

`show rmon alarm` reads it back: `alarm Variable = etherStatsPkts.11`, Delta, thresholds 100/10,
events 21/22, `alarm Startup Alarm = 1` (rising only, so no startup alarm can muddy the window),
owner ck33413.

The run (`work/s6-negative-control.log`), box clock:

    10:30:04  tcpdump -i eth3 -w s6trap.pcap udp port 162   (100 s)
    10:30:04 - 10:30:34  nothing done at all
    10:30:43 - 10:30:50  1000 frames, sudo vsend.py eth3 1000 ip10 --dst 02:00:00:00:44:08
                         --sip 10.38.22.33 --dip 10.38.22.40 --dport 9 --tag CK33413
    10:30:50 - 10:31:45  nothing done
    10:31:45  capture closed -- 2 packets captured

The decode, `snmpwire.py /tmp/cksnmpmon/s6trap.pcap --port 162 --values --abs`:

    #1   10:30:49.620 10.38.22.34:46366 -> 10.38.22.33:162  SNMPv2c  community='testing123' pdu=SNMPv2-Trap  msgData=PLAINTEXT
          1.3.6.1.2.1.1.3.0 = TimeTicks: 31369293
          1.3.6.1.6.3.1.1.4.1.0 = OID: 1.3.6.1.2.1.16.0.1   <- risingAlarm
          1.3.6.1.2.1.16.3.1.1.1.11 = INTEGER: 11                        alarmIndex
          1.3.6.1.2.1.16.3.1.1.3.11 = OID: 1.3.6.1.2.1.16.1.1.1.5.11     alarmVariable = etherStatsPkts.11
          1.3.6.1.2.1.16.3.1.1.4.11 = INTEGER: 2                         deltaValue
          1.3.6.1.2.1.16.3.1.1.5.11 = INTEGER: 945                       alarmValue
          1.3.6.1.2.1.16.3.1.1.7.11 = INTEGER: 100                       alarmRisingThreshold
    #2   10:31:09.634 ... risingAlarm's pair: 1.3.6.1.2.1.16.0.2 fallingAlarm, alarmIndex 11,
          etherStatsPkts.11, deltaValue, alarmValue 0, alarmFallingThreshold 10
    SUMMARY 2 SNMP message(s): 0 ENCRYPTED, 2 PLAINTEXT

**What that proves, in the one capture:**

- **The trap path is alive.** Two traps left the DUT, crossed port1.0.10, and were received at
  10.38.22.33:162 with the configured community. Agent, host configuration, community, routing
  and receiver are all working at that moment. 945 is the part of the 1000-frame burst that fell
  in the sample the alarm was on when it crossed.
- **No hardware-monitoring trap was emitted** in those 100 seconds, while
  `snmp-server enable trap hw-monitoring` was armed and all fourteen temperature sensors read
  Ok. `show system environment | include Temp|Overall` taken right after the window
  (`work/s7-envafter-groupremove.log`) still reports `Overall Status: Normal` and all fourteen
  sensors `Ok`, with readings within one degree of STEP 1.
- That is the correct behaviour for a fault trap on a healthy unit: **the measurement is "it
  stayed quiet when it should", not "we saw nothing".** What is NOT shown, and cannot be shown
  here, is the other half -- that it speaks when it should.

**STEP 5 PASS** (as a negative control).

