## STEP 7 -- the genuine over-temperature trap: NOT MEASURABLE, and reboot persistence: NOT MEASURED

🔴 **NOT MEASURABLE -- the trap this case is named after.** Stated plainly, with the reason:
there is no configurable temperature threshold on this platform (`temperature ?` and
`environment ?` are `% Unrecognized command`; no temperature group in
`snmp-server enable trap ?`; every limit in `show system environment` is reported by the
hardware), the coolest sensor sits 34 C below its limit, and **firing the real AT-ENVMONv2
hardware-monitoring fault trap requires physically heating the unit past a limit a bench cannot
move**. The dispatch's substitute -- an RMON alarm on the temperature OID -- was attempted in
four syntaxes and **refused by the product** (STEP 4), so that route is closed as well. No claim
is made anywhere in this log that a temperature trap was produced.

🔴 **NOT MEASURED -- reboot persistence.** This case performed no reload and wrote nothing to
startup-config. The L3 underlay the whole campaign depends on (vlan10 on port1.0.10, vlan48 on
port1.0.48) exists in running-config only -- the boot config is `flash:/default.cfg` -- so a
reload destroys the path to the trap receiver that the remaining cases need, and writing it to
startup-config is a change to the standing bench (STANDING-ORDERS §4), never taken unattended.
Already recorded in the queue as a NEEDS TEST ENGINEER item by group A; nothing new is asked.

**What would close each gap, for whoever reads this next:**

- the trap itself: a thermal chamber or a controlled heat source on the unit, or a developer
  build in which a sensor reading can be injected. Neither is a bench change;
- the RMON route: a product change lifting the alarmVariable restriction to any integer MIB
  object, which is what RFC 2819 specifies;
- persistence: the Test Engineer's decision to write the underlay to startup-config, after which
  every case in this campaign can also test reboot persistence.

